WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.120
Are you in the mood for to watch something&nbsp;
featuring wayward astronauts, robots on the fritz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.480
or cursed-filled murder investigations? Well,&nbsp;
you're in luck because the films of 2021 have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:16.160
exactly that, and more, to offer you! Keep&nbsp;
watching to discover 2021's best sci-fi movies.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.880
Stowaway is an existentially fraught&nbsp;
sci-fi drama that was released on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.160
Netflix back in April. The plot sends three&nbsp;
astronauts to Mars on a two-year mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.880
and then throws a major wrench into their&nbsp;
plans when a stowaway is discovered on-board.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.440
Director Joe Penna has assembled a great&nbsp;
cast. Toni Collette plays the ship's leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.960
on her final journey in space, Anna&nbsp;
Kendrick is the medical researcher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.880
and Daniel Dae Kim is the ship's&nbsp;
biologist. Unbeknownst to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.920
an unwitting, injured stowaway played by&nbsp;
Shamier [shuh-MEER] Anderson is also onboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.880
and whatever caused his predicament has also&nbsp;
damaged the ship's life-support controls, leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.960
enough oxygen for only three people. One must go,&nbsp;
or they all die. So the question is: who takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.840
that final spacewalk? Variety's Owen Gleiberman&nbsp;
[GLEE-ber-muhn] had high praise as he declared,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.800
"Penna takes a mission to Mars and unfurls&nbsp;
it on a direct and intimate emotional level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.280
We always feel the characters could be us."

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.720
Meanwhile, Christy Lemire&nbsp;
of RogerEbert.com called it:

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.400
"A clever take on a familiar genre&nbsp;
[ZHAHN-ruh] with a terrific cast."

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.800
So if you're in the mood for a sci-fi flick&nbsp;
that'll challenge you philosophically and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.120
you wonder what you would do in a life-threatening&nbsp;
situation, then you better check out Stowaway.

00:01:28.862 --> 00:01:31.520
The Mitchells vs. the Machines is a&nbsp;
kid-friendly animated adventure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.200
hit Netflix on April 30. It features the voices&nbsp;
of Abbi Jacobson, Danny McBride, Maya Rudolph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.400
and Michael Rianda [ree-AN-duh],&nbsp;
who also directed and co-wrote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.680
They play a family on a road trip that turns into&nbsp;
an apocalyptic fight against a robotic revolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.360
The emphasis on technology-as-villain grounds the&nbsp;
film squarely in sci-fi territory as the Mitchells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.720
are thrust into a cell phone war after they've set&nbsp;
aside their gadgets for an old-fashioned vacation.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.280
The Mitchells vs. the Machines&nbsp;
was a hit with critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.560
and it was also produced by the&nbsp;
high-powered team of Phil Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.640
and Christopher Miller. Bob Strauss of&nbsp;
the San Francisco Chronicle called it:

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.200
"A machine apocalypse for the whole family."

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.360
Meanwhile, The Guardian's Benjamin Lee noted,

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:17.120
"Rianda makes an earnest stab at trying to offer&nbsp;
up a balanced view of the pros and cons of iPhone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.680
culture. Rather than the easier and clumsier&nbsp;
film he could have made scolding kids for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.240
swiping and tapping and missing out on real life,&nbsp;
he shows that young digital creators are achieving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.520
incredible things and that, when used right, this&nbsp;
form of connectedness can be a powerful force."

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.120
Oxygen, the latest from director Alexandre Aja&nbsp;
["Alexander" AH-zhuh], is being hailed as a taut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.240
engaging thriller. In this French-language film,&nbsp;
a woman named Liz wakes up in a cryogenic pod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.800
with no knowledge of who or where&nbsp;
she is. Her only companion is an AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.120
who advises her that the oxygen levels of&nbsp;
the pod are decreasing at alarming levels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.120
If Liz can't figure out what's going&nbsp;
on fast enough, she'll suffocate.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.760
Aja is best known for creature features like&nbsp;
Piranha 3D and Crawl, but this time around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.520
he's earning praise for his restraint. Filmed&nbsp;
during COVID lockdown, Oxygen eschews his usual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.480
viscera for more existential dread. As David&nbsp;
Rooney of The Hollywood Reporter declared,

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.480
"While cult followers of Aja's extreme&nbsp;
horror might be let down by his turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.480
toward more measured sci-fi, there's&nbsp;
plenty of creepy unease packed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.880
his execution of a simple scenario&nbsp;
that grows increasingly complicated."

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.680
Special praise must all be given to lead actress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.200
Mélanie Laurent [MEL-uh-nee luh-RONT]; in a&nbsp;
movie that's totally focused on one person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.960
you need an emotional, powerhouse&nbsp;
performance, and that's what she delivers.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.320
A Quiet Place Part II ["two"] is the&nbsp;
long-awaited sequel to the 2018 original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:44.320
that had its theatrical-exclusive release&nbsp;
on May 28, 2021. This creepy sci-fi flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.680
was quickly certified fresh at Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;
earning praise from critics and audiences alike.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.680
Director-screenwriter-star John Krasinski's&nbsp;
[kruh-ZIN-skee] continuation of the Abbott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.640
family's story provides a glimpse of the first&nbsp;
day that the sonic aliens arrived. Krasinski's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.840
real-life wife Emily Blunt returns as mom&nbsp;
Evelyn, who journeys with her newborn infant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.720
two older children in search of a new place to&nbsp;
live after their farm becomes uninhabitable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.040
They eventually come across Cillian [KIL-ee-uhn]&nbsp;
Murphy's Emmett, a friendly acquaintance briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.280
seen in the opening events of the film.&nbsp;
Alas, Emmett is now withdrawn and sullen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.800
and our heroes don't know if they can&nbsp;
trust him, but they need his help.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.320
A Quiet Place Part II has earned praise for its&nbsp;
superior use of tension and its tight script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.080
The performances are incredible across&nbsp;
the board, with Bloody Disgusting's Meagan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.200
Navarro lavishing specific praise on deaf actress&nbsp;
Millicent [MIL-uh-suhnt] Simmonds by declaring,

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.160
"This is above all Simmonds'&nbsp;
show, and she nails it."

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.280
If you were worried whether or not the&nbsp;
sequel would live up to the first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.880
you can rest assured that it's&nbsp;
another scary, silent treat.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.383
"Run."

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:56.000
Chinese-Taiwanese sci-fi mystery flick&nbsp;
The Soul arrived on Netflix on April 14.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.200
It doesn't have a lot of reviews in the U.S., but&nbsp;
it's nevertheless hitting a chord with audiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.240
Set in a near future when cars hum with&nbsp;
electricity and holograms pop out of buildings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.600
The Soul tells the story of a prosecutor who has&nbsp;
cancer and whose detective wife is newly pregnant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.560
Despite his illness, they work together to solve&nbsp;
the murder of a tycoon. Was it his young wife who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.760
killed him, or his alienated son, or his partner,&nbsp;
who's invented a way to revive dying neurons with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.200
a special powder? Or is it the result of a curse&nbsp;
that his first wife placed on him before she died?

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.680
The story focuses on the interactions of&nbsp;
the characters amidst the sci-fi and occult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.880
trappings. Daniel Hart of&nbsp;
Ready Steady Cuts declared,

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.720
"This is a real, well-woven mystery&nbsp;
that wants to provoke the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.000
with thoughts rather than events,&nbsp;
which makes it even more fascinating."

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.280
Meanwhile, JK Sooja of Common Sense Media opined,

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.840
"This film [has] slick production, strong acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.480
and [an] intricate plot. Quite simply, the&nbsp;
central mystery in The Soul is captivating."

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.400
Ryan Reynolds is the inimitable star of Free&nbsp;
Guy, which arrived in theaters back in August.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:03.280
He plays a guy named Guy, who spends every single&nbsp;
day greeting his goldfish, drinking coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.680
and going to his job at the bank, where he talks&nbsp;
with his best friend, security guard Buddy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:13.120
played by Lil Rel Howery. But Guy has no idea&nbsp;
that he's stuck in a predetermined loop as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.880
non-player character in a popular video game.&nbsp;
That is, until he meets Molotov Girl, aka Millie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.160
and becomes enamored with her. Millie, played&nbsp;
by Jodie Comer, wants to prove that powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.680
video game impresario Antwan stole the game that&nbsp;
she developed with her friend Keys. Can Guy help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:35.040
Millie get proof before Antwan destroys the game,&nbsp;
as well as Guy, Buddy, and all their NPC friends?

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.480
Since its release, Free Guy has&nbsp;
been earning raves from critics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.720
Charlotte O'Sullivan of The Standard,&nbsp;
for one, praised its humor, as she said,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.160
"[It's] knowing rather than nasty and, even at its&nbsp;
most surreal, avoids coming across as indulgent."

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.280
Meanwhile, USA Today's Brian Truitt called it a:

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:54.480
"Big, warm hug of a movie with plenty to love."

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.280
And Robert Daniels of The&nbsp;
Playlist declared simply,

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:58.720
"Free Guy is a winner."

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:02.640
"Is that a Glock in your pocket?"&nbsp;
"No." "What?!" "It's two Glocks."

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:09.040
Director James delivered his take on DC&nbsp;
super-villains in 2021 with The Suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:15.440
Squad. Less a sequel to 2016's Suicide Squad and&nbsp;
more a reboot, the film brings back Harley Quinn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:20.720
Amanda Waller, Captain Boomerang, and Rick Flag,&nbsp;
while adding a whole slew of new characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.160
including the likes of Bloodsport, Peacemaker,&nbsp;
Ratcatcher 2, Polka-Dot Man, and King Shark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.040
And that's just barely scratching the surface.&nbsp;
Once again, Waller offers a ragtag group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.680
supervillains the chance to shave time off their&nbsp;
prison sentences by completing a secret mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:40.400
for her. If they go off script, she'll blow off&nbsp;
their heads. What follows is a lot of mayhem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:45.360
a lot of swearing, and a lot of gory fun.&nbsp;
The Seattle Times' Soren Andersen raved,

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.320
"Gunn masterfully mixes humor and bloodshed and&nbsp;
manages to give a surprising number of characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.200
room to develop their personas. And when it&nbsp;
comes to staging set pieces, he's at his best."

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.560
Meagan Navarro of Bloody&nbsp;
Disgusting concurred, as she wrote,

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.800
"It's endearingly sweet, even as it's spilling&nbsp;
brain matter and gore by the bucket load."

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:03.515
"Bird."

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.080
Filmed on an iPhone during the COVID-19&nbsp;
lockdown, British film Infinitum:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.760
Subject Unknown features themes&nbsp;
both timely and timeless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:17.840
Written by married couple Matthew and&nbsp;
Tori Butler-Hart and directed by Matthew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:23.680
it stars Tori as Jane, an amnesiac woman trapped&nbsp;
in a parallel world and a time loop. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:28.880
features cameos from Sir Ian McKellen and Conleth&nbsp;
Hill of Game of Thrones, both of whom may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:34.240
information that could help Jane regain her memory&nbsp;
and return to her own world before time runs out.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:38.720
Infinitum: Subject Unknown has been praised&nbsp;
specifically for Tori's performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:41.440
Rachael Harper at SciFiNow, for one, wrote,

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.440
"She shoulders [the] responsibility [of&nbsp;
being alone on screen] incredibly well."

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.160
While Keri O'Shea of Warped Perspective raved,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:52.320
"[Bulter-Hart] pitches [her acting] perfectly&nbsp;
given the situations unfolding around her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.640
and she does most of it without saying anything."

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.800
The film is notable for how it tackles its&nbsp;
heavy and horrific themes in a surreal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.520
unique manner. So if you're looking for a sci-fi&nbsp;
flick that's more on the intense side of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.840
then definitely add Infinitum:&nbsp;
Subject Unknown to your watch list.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:14.000
Frank Herbert's 1965 novel Dune has long been&nbsp;
considered unfilmable, but people keep trying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.120
There was the 1984 David Lynch version,&nbsp;
followed by the 2000 TV miniseries, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:24.640
its latest cinematic incarnation is a sumptuous&nbsp;
feast for the eyes and ears. Director Denis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:28.480
Villeneuve [den-NEE vill-NUHV] wisely presents&nbsp;
only a portion of the source material in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:33.040
that's both satisfying as a movie in itself&nbsp;
and as an introduction of the events to come.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:37.760
It's the year 10191, and House Atreides&nbsp;
[ah-TRAY-dees] is tasked by the Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.960
to replace the ruthless Harkonnens&nbsp;
[HAR-kuh-nens] as overseers of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:44.560
planet Arrakis [uh-RAH-kis], harvesting the&nbsp;
planet's coveted spice and keeping an eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:48.640
on the Fremen [FREM-uhn], the indigenous&nbsp;
population whom the Harkonnens brutally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.200
oppressed. The Harkonnens are less than&nbsp;
pleased, and conflict ensues. Audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.600
and critics alike have fallen in love with&nbsp;
Dune. Salon's Melanie McFarland called it:

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.960
"A gorgeous sensory immersion that&nbsp;
holds us in its spell for hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.920
with an ending that sparks&nbsp;
yearning to see what comes next."

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.160
The Houston Chronicle's Cary Darling raved,

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:07.760
"Dune is an impressive feat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:12.720
a plunge into a richly designed world that&nbsp;
feels at once ancient and futuristic."

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.920
And The A.V. Club's Ignatiy Vishnevetsky&nbsp;
[ig-NAH-tee vish-nuh-VET-skee] noted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.560
although the story may feel "inconclusive" as is,

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:23.760
"The resulting film is a rarity among the overlong&nbsp;
effects-heavy blockbusters of the last decade:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.880
One actually wishes it&nbsp;
didn't have to end so soon."

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.720
"Dune is very rich. It's an adventure&nbsp;
movie. It's a coming-of-age movie."

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:36.320
A basic "meaning of true friendship" tale&nbsp;
gets a digital upgrade in the animated feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:42.640
Ron's Gone Wrong. Lonely Barney, voiced by Jack&nbsp;
Dylan Grazer, gets his very own B-bot named Ron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:47.040
voiced by Zach Galifianakis [gal-ih-fuh-NAK-is].&nbsp;
B-bots are advertised as a kid's "best friend out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:52.480
of the box" that connects to all their favorite&nbsp;
apps. What could possibly go wrong? Well, Ron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.960
could go wrong. Barney's father and grandmother&nbsp;
can't afford the expensive companion, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.760
luck into one that's literally fallen off a&nbsp;
truck. The damage from the fall causes Ron to act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:06.480
unlike any other B-bot, which soon comes to the&nbsp;
attention of Bubble, the company that sells them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:12.480
Bubble's COO Andrew isn't pleased, and it's not&nbsp;
long before Bubble is after Ron to destroy him.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:17.280
While its box office receipts haven't been&nbsp;
great, Ron's Gone Wrong has impressed almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.200
everyone who bought a ticket to see it. Angie&nbsp;
Han [HAHN] at The Hollywood Reporter noted,

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.200
"What [it] lacks in daring imagination,&nbsp;
it makes up for with endearing good humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.720
thoughtful cultural critique,&nbsp;
and one heck of a cute robot."

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:33.280
And The Washington Post's Pat Padua&nbsp;
praised the film's family appeal, noting,

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:38.160
"Ron's Gone Wrong has plenty of slapstick and&nbsp;
potty humor for kids. But adults will also be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:43.280
intrigued by its frequently scathing (albeit&nbsp;
somewhat conflicted) critique of consumerism."

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.640
Simply put, you'll be wrong if you&nbsp;
don't check out Ron's Gone Wrong.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:49.040
"Friend request. Friend request."

